  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4.$\nAlso, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\cdot 2(10)=-80{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is decreasing at  $80\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$",
